\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces (a) Held-out PPL as a function of quality threshold $\tau $ at $\rho \in \{0.4, 0.6\}$. $\tau {=}0.7$ and $\tau {=}0.8$ yield comparable holdout PPL at $\rho {=}0.4$; at $\rho {=}0.6$, $\tau {=}0.7$ is statistically better (paired $t{=}{-}4.70$, $p{<}0.05$, 3 seeds, $\Delta {=}{-}0.005$). $\tau {=}0.7$ also achieves lower OOD perplexity at both levels ($\rho {=}0.6$: $\Delta _{\text  {OWT}}{=}{-}0.014$, $\Delta _{\text  {Wiki}}{=}{-}0.013$, 2 seeds). (b) Normalized PPL gap relative to uniform at shared $\tau $ values.}}{12}{figure.caption.41}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:tau_rho_rank}{{10}{12}{(a) Held-out PPL as a function of quality threshold $\tau $ at $\rho \in \{0.4, 0.6\}$. $\tau {=}0.7$ and $\tau {=}0.8$ yield comparable holdout PPL at $\rho {=}0.4$; at $\rho {=}0.6$, $\tau {=}0.7$ is statistically better (paired $t{=}{-}4.70$, $p{<}0.05$, 3 seeds, $\Delta {=}{-}0.005$). $\tau {=}0.7$ also achieves lower OOD perplexity at both levels ($\rho {=}0.6$: $\Delta _{\text {OWT}}{=}{-}0.014$, $\Delta _{\text {Wiki}}{=}{-}0.013$, 2 seeds). (b) Normalized PPL gap relative to uniform at shared $\tau $ values}{figure.caption.41}{}}
